/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Columns are grouped in four independent ways.
   - The frame matters too.
   - The unsupported length l is the clear height between the floor and the underside of the beam (or slab) that holds the column sideways.
   - For a column in a real frame, K comes from the stiffness of the beams meeting at each end (the charts in IS 456 Annex E).
   - D is the section depth for bending about the major (x) axis and b the width for bending about the minor (y) axis, both in mm; lex and ley are the effective lengths for buckling about those axes, in mm.
   - Check both axes.
   - Pu is the factored axial load; Puz = 0.45fckAc + 0.75fyAsc is the pure axial capacity; Pb is the axial load at the balanced point (concrete crushing and tension steel yielding together).
   - Even a short column is never loaded exactly through its centroid.
   - l is the unsupported length in mm and D is the lateral dimension in the direction of the eccentricity, in mm.
*/
